comments.body:
@participant3 @participant4
Thank you both for taking this up.

@participant3
Would it be possible to share a Colab gist demonstrating how random rotation behaves, especially for segmentation tasks? In particular, it would be helpful to visually inspect the effect on segmentation masks. As you know, due to border effects, the boundary regions can be sensitive in segmentation, where newly introduced border pixels (typically black) may be treated as the background class, which might not be desirable. I’m not entirely sure whether this behavior is acceptable in practice, so a visual example would be very useful.

@participant4 @participant3
Adding code-level documentation would be a great improvement. That said, when users first evaluate an API, they typically rely on the API documentation rather than diving into the source code. Currently, as seen [here](https://keras.io/api/layers/preprocessing_layers/image_augmentation/), most APIs lack visual demonstrations. Providing component-level examples or a global usage guide with visual demonstrations would be extremely helpful and effective. You might find inspiration from the PyTorch documentation in this regard.

@participant2
Is there a benchmark script available to evaluate the inference speed of the Keras augmentation layers?

@participant3 
That sounds fine.

@participant6 
>  Do we want to have something like a resize_to_original flag to resize it to the original size if true?

I think that wouldn't be necessary with the latest suggestion made by @participant3 , right?

@participant6 
Maybe. I assumed the padding might addressed that, not sure. 

@participant5 
If you're open, you can create a guide in keras-io regarding the usage of augmentation layers. However, I think, instead of guide, an example for per augmentation to each of its own page would be more beneficial. For example, [albumentaitonx](https://albumentations.ai/docs/3-basic-usage/bounding-boxes-augmentations/)
